Human, Simple Speech for Your Demo

Hi everyone, thanks for joining. Today I want to walk you through the Playwright automation framework I built for our F5 project. I’ll keep everything very simple because I know not everyone here works with Playwright on a daily basis, and that’s totally fine. My goal is just to show how this framework works, why it’s useful for us, and how it will help our team in the long run.

So first, what this framework actually is: I created a UI automation setup using Playwright that allows us to run tests across multiple browsers — Chromium, Firefox, and WebKit — all from one single codebase. It’s fast, it’s stable, and it’s designed so it can easily fit into CI/CD pipelines later. Basically, it helps us make sure our web flows behave correctly and don’t break when new changes go in.

Why Playwright? The main reason is reliability. Playwright has built-in auto-waiting, which means our tests don’t need those manual sleep timers that usually cause flakiness. It also gives us really good debugging tools right out of the box — things like trace files, videos, and screenshots — so when a test fails, we can understand exactly what happened. And since it supports TypeScript and JavaScript very well, it’s easier for developers to read and contribute to the tests.

In the repo, everything is structured to be clear and easy to navigate. Our test files sit under the tests folder, and the shared data and design tokens are organized under design-tokens, so our visual checks stay consistent with our design system. The config is already prepared to run headless and in parallel, which means it finishes quickly and is ready for CI.

What this gives us as a team is faster feedback when we make changes. Tests run in parallel, they produce helpful reports, and they don’t suffer from the usual flaky behavior that slows QA work down. It also gives us broad browser coverage without extra work, and the framework itself is developer-friendly, so anyone on the team can pick it up without a steep learning curve. Another really useful part is visual testing — this helps us catch UI regressions early, especially important for the CMS project.

This framework adds value because it protects our key user journeys before we release anything. Login, navigation flows, homepage behavior — all of this can be automatically checked. The standardized structure also makes onboarding new team members easier and prevents us from writing duplicate tests. And once we plug the framework into CI, it will automatically block regressions, attach trace and video artifacts for failed tests, and give us quick triage.

A typical test is very straightforward: we set up the page and data, perform an action, and then assert the expected result using Playwright’s expect API. If anything goes wrong, Playwright automatically saves the trace, video, and screenshot, so we never lose context.

Getting started is simple: install the dependencies, run a sample test, open the report, and check the trace viewer if something fails. After today’s demo, anyone from the team who wants to try it can easily do so.

As next steps, I plan to automate a few of our most important flows first, like the critical user journeys. Then we’ll continue building page objects and helper functions to make the framework even easier to use. And once the team is comfortable, we can fully integrate it into CI so tests run on every pull request. I’m also happy to do pairing sessions with anyone who wants to learn how to write a test or analyze a trace file.

So that’s the overview. I’m excited to show this to you today because I really believe this framework will help improve quality, speed, and confidence in everything we release. And for me personally, it’s important because this shows the value automation can bring to the team and how I can continue contributing to the project in a meaningful way.
